aderboard/figure-1-leaderboard-NWJTV4ZV.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;">=
</div><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption cl=
ass=3D"py-1">Figure 1 Leaderboard</figcaption></div></figure>
<h2 id=3D"step-1---understand-the-problem-and-establish-design-scope">Step =
1 - Understand the Problem and Establish Design Scope</h2>
<p>Leaderboards can be pretty straightforward, but there are a number of di=
fferent matters that can add complexity. We should clarify the requirements=
.</p>
<p><strong>Candidate:</strong> How is the score calculated for the leaderbo=
ard?<br>
<strong>Interviewer:</strong> The user gets a point when they win a match. =
We can go with a simple point system in which each user has a score associa=
ted with them. Each time the user wins a match, we should add a point to th=
eir total score.</p>
<p><strong>Candidate:</strong> Are all players included in the leaderboard?=
<br>
<strong>Interviewer:</strong> Yes.</p>
<p><strong>Candidate:</strong> Is there a time segment associated with the =
leaderboard?<br>
<strong>Interviewer:</strong> Each month, a new tournament kicks off which =
starts a new leaderboard.</p>
<p><strong>Candidate:</strong> Can we assume we only care about the top 10 =
users?<br>
<strong>Interviewer:</strong> We want to display the top 10 users as well a=
s the position of a specific user on the leaderboard. If time allows, let=
=E2=80=99s also discuss how to return users who are four places above and b=
elow a specific user.</p>

<p><strong>Candidate:</strong> How many users are in a tournament?<br>
<strong>Interviewer:</strong> Average of 5 million daily active users (DAU)=
 and 25 million monthly active users (MAU).</p>
<p><strong>Candidate:</strong> How many matches are played on average durin=
g a tournament?<br>
<strong>Interviewer:</strong> Each player plays 10 matches per day on avera=
ge.</p>
<p><strong>Candidate:</strong> How do we determine the rank if two players =
have the same score?<br>
<strong>Interviewer:</strong> In this case, their ranks are the same. If ti=
me allows, we can talk about ways to break ties.</p>
<p><strong>Candidate:</strong> Does the leaderboard need to be real-time?<b=
r>
<strong>Interviewer:</strong> Yes, we want to present real-time results, or=
 as close as possible. It is not okay to present a batched history of resul=
ts.</p>
<p>Now that we=E2=80=99ve gathered all the requirements, let=E2=80=99s list=
 the functional requirements.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Display top 10 players on the leaderboard.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Show a user=E2=80=99s specific rank.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Display players who are four places above and below the desired user (bo=
nus).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Other than clarifying functional requirements, it=E2=80=99s important to=
 understand non-functional requirements.</p>
<p><strong>Non-functional requirements</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Real-time update on scores.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Score update is reflected on the leaderboard in real-time.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>General scalability, availability, and reliability requirements.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"back-of-the-envelope-estimation">Back-of-the-envelope estimation<=
/h3>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s take a look at some back-of-the-envelope calculations to d=
etermine the potential scale and challenges our solution will need to addre=
ss.</p>
<p>With 5 million DAU, if the game had an even distribution of players duri=
ng a 24-hour period, we would have an average of 50 users per second (5,000=
,000 DAU / 10^5 seconds =3D ~50). However, we know that usages most likely =
aren=E2=80=99t evenly distributed, and potentially there are peaks during e=
venings when many people across different time zones have time to play. To =
account for this, we could assume that peak load would be 5 times the avera=
ge. Therefore we=E2=80=99d want to allow for a peak load of 250 users per s=
econd.</p>
<p>QPS for users scoring a point: if a user plays 10 games per day on avera=
ge, the QPS for users scoring a point is: 50 * 10 =3D ~500. Peak QPS is 5x =
of the average: 500 * 5 =3D 2,500.</p>
<p>QPS for fetching the top 10 leaderboard: assume a user opens the game on=
ce a day and the top 10 leaderboard is loaded only when a user first opens =
the game. The QPS for this is around 50.</p>
<h2 id=3D"step-2---propose-high-level-design-and-get-buy-in">Step 2 - Propo=
se High-Level Design and Get Buy-In</h2>
<p>In this section, we will discuss API design, high-level architecture, an=
d data models.</p>
<h3 id=3D"api-design">API design</h3>
<p>At a high level, we need the following three APIs:</p>
<p><strong>POST /v1/scores</strong></p>
<p>Update a user=E2=80=99s position on the leaderboard when a user wins a g=
ame. The request parameters are listed below. This should be an internal AP=
I that can only be called by the game servers. The client should not be abl=
e to update the leaderboard score directly.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th><strong>Field</strong></th><th><strong>Description</s=
trong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>user_id</td><td>The user who wins a =
game.</td></tr><tr><td>points</td><td>The number of points a user gained by=
 winning a game.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p class=3D"tableCaption">Table 1</p>
<p>Response:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th><strong>Name</strong></th><th><strong>Description</st=
rong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200 OK</td><td>Successfully updated a=
 user=E2=80=99s score.</td></tr><tr><td>400 Bad Request</td><td>Failed to u=
pdate a user=E2=80=99s score.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p class=3D"tableCaption">Table 2</p>
<p><strong>GET /v1/scores</strong></p>
<p>Fetch the top 10 players from the leaderboard.</p>
<p>Sample response:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "data": [
    {
      "user_id": "user_id1",
      "user_name": "alice",
      "rank": 1,
      "score": 12543
    },
    {
      "user_id": "user_id2",
      "user_name": "bob",
      "rank": 2,
      "score": 11500
    }
  ],
  ...
  "total": 10
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>GET /v1/scores/{:user_id}</strong></p>
<p>Fetch the rank of a specific user.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th><strong>Field</strong></th><th><strong>Description</s=
trong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>user_id</td><td>The ID of the user w=
hose rank we would like to fetch.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p class=3D"tableCaption">Table 3</p>
<p>Sample response:</p>
<pre><code>{
    "user_info": {
        "user_id": "user5",
        "score": 1000,
        "rank": 6,
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h3 id=3D"high-level-architecture">High-level architecture</h3>
<p>The high-level design diagram is shown in Figure 2. There are two servic=
es in this design. The game service allows users to play the game and the l=
eaderboard service creates and displays a leaderboard.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a system architecture diagram illustrating =
the flow of information in a leaderboard system for a game.  The diagram be=
gins with a top-level box depicting a user (represented by a person icon) i=
nteracting with a mobile device (represented by a phone icon).  This user i=
nitiates two main actions:  '1) Win a game,' which triggers a flow to the '=
Game service' box.  The 'Game service' then sends an '2) Update score' mess=
age to the 'Leaderboard service' box.  Simultaneously, the user can also re=
quest '4 a. Get leaderboard' and '4 b. Get player_rank' from the 'Leaderboa=
rd service.'  The 'Leaderboard service' receives the score update and proce=
sses it, subsequently updating the 'Leaderboard Store' (represented by a da=
tabase cylinder) via a '3) Update score' action.  The numbered circles indi=
cate the sequential order of operations, while the lettered items under num=
ber 4 represent sub-actions within a single user request.  The arrows show =
the direction of data flow between the components." loading=3D"lazy" width=
=3D"360" height=3D"404" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://b=
ytebytego.com/images/courses/system-design-interview/real-time-gaming-leade=
rboard/figure-2-high-level-design-XQWDCF72.svg" style=3D"color: transparent=
;"></div><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption=
 class=3D"py-1">Figure 2 High-level design</figcaption></div></figure>
<ol>
<li>
<p>When a player wins a game, the client sends a request to the game servic=
e.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The game service ensures the win is valid and calls the leaderboard serv=
ice to update the score.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The leaderboard service updates the user=E2=80=99s score in the leaderbo=
ard store.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>A player makes a call to the leaderboard service directly to fetch leade=
rboard data, including:</p>
<p>a. top 10 leaderboard.</p>
<p>b. the rank of the player on the leaderboard.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Before settling on this design, we considered a few alternatives and dec=
ided against them. It might be helpful to go through the thought process of=
 this and to compare different options.</p>
<p><strong>Should the client talk to the leaderboard service directly?</str=
ong></p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents two alternative system designs for updating=
 a leaderboard after a game is won.  The 'Current Option' shows a sequentia=
l process: a user (represented by a person icon and a phone icon) wins a ga=
me (step 1), triggering a call to the 'Game service.'  The 'Game service' t=
hen updates the score (step 2) by calling the 'Leaderboard service.'  The '=
Alternative Option' presents a different approach: the user's action (repre=
sented by the same user icon and phone icon) directly sets a score (step 1)=
. This action directly calls the 'Leaderboard service' to update the score =
(step 2), bypassing the intermediary 'Game service.'  Both diagrams use num=
bered steps to indicate the flow of information and actions.  The dashed li=
nes around each option delineate the separate design proposals." loading=3D=
"lazy" width=3D"500" height=3D"419" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=
=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/system-design-interview/real-time=
-gaming-leaderboard/figure-3-who-sets-the-leaderboard-score-O6AIP5UI.svg" s=
tyle=3D"color: transparent;"></div><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-con=
tent: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 3 Who sets the leaderboard=
 score</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>In the alternative design, the score is set by the client. This option i=
s not secure because it is subject to man-in-the-middle attack [x], where p=
layers can put in a proxy and change scores at will. Therefore, we need the=
 score to be set on the server-side.</p>
<p>Note that for server authoritative games such as online poker, the clien=
t may not need to call the game server explicitly to set scores. The game s=
erver handles all game logic, and it knows when the game finishes and could=
 set the score without any client intervention.</p>
<p><strong>Do we need a message queue between the game service and the lead=
erboard service?</strong></p>
<p>The answer to this question highly depends on how the game scores are us=
ed. If the data is used in other places or supports multiple functionalitie=
s, then it might make sense to put data in Kafka as shown in Figure 4. This=
 way, the same data can be consumed by multiple consumers, such as leaderbo=
ard service, analytics service, push notification service, etc. This is esp=
ecially true when the game is a turn-based or multi-player game in which we=
 need to notify other players about the score update. As this is not an exp=
licit requirement based on the conversation with the interviewer, we do not=
 use a message queue in our design.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a system architecture diagram illustrating =
data flow using Apache Kafka as a message broker.  A 'Game service' compone=
nt produces data, which is sent as messages to a central 'Kafka' component.=
  Kafka acts as a distributed streaming platform, receiving and storing the=
se messages.  From Kafka, the data is then consumed by three separate servi=
ces: a 'Leaderboard service,' an 'Analytic service,' and a 'Push Notificati=
on service.'  Each of these consumer services receives a stream of messages=
 from Kafka, allowing for parallel processing and independent updates to th=
e leaderboard, analytics, and user notifications based on the game data.  T=
he arrows indicate the unidirectional flow of data from the producer (Game =
service) to Kafka, and then from Kafka to each of the three consumer servic=
es." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"500" height=3D"182" decoding=3D"async" data-=
nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/system-design-inter=
view/real-time-gaming-leaderboard/figure-4-game-scores-are-used-by-multiple=
-services-UWBWILEE.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div><div style=3D"d=
isplay: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 4=
 Game scores are used by multiple services</figcaption></div></figure>
<h3 id=3D"data-models">Data models</h3>
<p>One of the key components in the system is the leaderboard store. We wil=
l discuss three potential solutions: relational database, Redis, and NoSQL =
(NoSQL solution is explained in deep dive).</p>
<h4 id=3D"relational-database-solution">Relational database solution</h4>
<p>First, let=E2=80=99s take a step back and start with the simplest soluti=
on. What if the scale doesn=E2=80=99t matter and we have only a few users?<=
/p>
<p>We would most likely opt to have a simple leaderboard solution using a r=
elational database system (RDS). Each monthly leaderboard could be represen=
ted as a database table containing user id and score columns. When the user=
 wins a match, either award the user 1 point if they are new, or increase t=
heir existing score by 1 point. To determine a user=E2=80=99s ranking on th=
e leaderboard, we would sort the table by the score in descending order. Th=
e details are explained below.</p>
<p>Leaderboard DB table:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a simple database table schema diagram for =
a leaderboard. The diagram is a rectangular box with a header and a body. T=
he header, positioned at the top, boldly displays the table name 'leaderboa=
rd'.  The body, separated from the header by a horizontal line, contains tw=
o rows defining the table's columns. The first row specifies a column named=
 'user_id' with a data type of 'varchar,' indicating it will store variable=
-length strings representing user identifiers. The second row defines a col=
umn named 'score' with a data type of 'int,' signifying it will store integ=
er values representing user scores.  No connections or information flow is =
depicted beyond the table's internal structure." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"=
335" height=3D"218" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://byteb=
ytego.com/_next/image?url=3D%2Fimages%2Fcourses%2Fsystem-design-interview%2=
Freal-time-gaming-leaderboard%2Ffigure-5-leaderboard-table-WGJD5HTB.png&amp=
;w=3D3840&amp;q=3D75" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div><div style=3D"dis=
play: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 5 L=
eaderboard table</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>In reality, the leaderboard table has additional information, such as a =
game_id, a timestamp, etc. However, the underlying logic of how to query an=
d update the leaderboard remains the same. For simplicity, we assume only t=
he current month=E2=80=99s leaderboard data is stored in the leaderboard ta=
ble.</p>
<p><strong>A user wins a point:</strong></p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a simplified system architecture diagram sh=
owing data flow between a Leaderboard service and a database.  A rectangula=
r box labeled 'Leaderboard service' is depicted on the left, representing a=
 software component responsible for managing leaderboard data. A directed a=
rrow originates from this box, indicating data flow.  The arrow is labeled =
'Insert or update DB,' signifying that the Leaderboard service sends data t=
o the database for insertion or updating.  This arrow connects to a cylindr=
ical shape on the right, representing a database (DB), which receives and s=
tores the leaderboard data. The overall diagram illustrates a unidirectiona=
l data flow from the Leaderboard service to the database, suggesting a proc=
ess where the service updates the persistent storage with leaderboard infor=
mation." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"500" height=3D"74" decoding=3D"async" da=
ta-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/system-design-in=
terview/real-time-gaming-leaderboard/figure-6-a-user-wins-a-point-FN3VKT66.=
svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div><div style=3D"display: flex; justi=
fy-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 6 A user wins a poin=
t</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>Assume every score update would be an increment of 1. If a user doesn=E2=
=80=99t yet have an entry in the leaderboard for the month, the first inser=
t would be:</p>
<pre><code>INSERT INTO leaderboard (user_id, score) VALUES ('mary1934', 1);
</code></pre>
<p>An update to the user=E2=80=99s score would be:</p>
<pre><code>UPDATE leaderboard set score=3Dscore + 1 where user_id=3D'mary19=
34';
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Find a user=E2=80=99s leaderboard position:</strong></p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a simplified system architecture diagram sh=
owing data retrieval for a leaderboard.  A rectangular box labeled 'Leaderb=
oard service' is depicted on the left, representing a service responsible f=
or managing and displaying leaderboard information.  A directed arrow exten=
ds from this box to the right, labeled 'Fetch from DB, sorted by rank,' ind=
icating that the service retrieves data from a database.  This arrow connec=
ts to a cylindrical shape on the right, representing a database (DB). The a=
rrow and label clearly show that the data fetched from the database is sort=
ed by rank before being presumably used by the Leaderboard service.  The ov=
erall diagram illustrates a simple data flow from a database to a service r=
esponsible for presenting a ranked leaderboard." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"=
500" height=3D"79" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://byteby=
tego.com/images/courses/system-design-interview/real-time-gaming-leaderboar=
d/figure-7-find-a-users-leaderboard-position-6WVQDZK3.svg" style=3D"color: =
transparent;"></div><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center;">=
<figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 7 Find a user=E2=80=99s leaderboard posit=
ion</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>To fetch the user rank, we would sort the leaderboard table and rank by =
the score:</p>
<pre><code>SELECT (@rownum :=3D @rownum + 1) AS rank, user_id, score
FROM leaderboard
ORDER BY score DESC;
</code></pre>
<p>The result of the SQL query looks like this:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th><strong>rank</strong></th><th><strong>user_id</strong=
></th><th><strong>score</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>=
happy_tomato</td><td>987</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>mallow</td><td>902</td>=
</tr><tr><td>3</td><td>smith</td><td>870</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>mary193=
4</td><td>850</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p class=3D"tableCaption">Table 4 Result sorted by score</p>
<p>This solution works when the data set is small, but the query becomes ve=
ry slow when there are millions of rows. Let=E2=80=99s take a look at why.<=
/p>
<p>To figure out the rank of a user, we need to sort every single player in=
to their correct spot on the leaderboard so we can determine exactly what t=
he correct rank is. Remember that there can be duplicate scores as well, so=
 the rank isn=E2=80=99t just the position of the user in the list.</p>
<p>SQL databases are not performant when we have to process large amounts o=
f continuously changing information. Attempting to do a rank operation over=
 millions of rows is going to take 10s of seconds, which is not acceptable =
for the desired real-time approach. Since the data is constantly changing, =
it is also not feasible to consider a cache.</p>
<p>A relational database is not designed to handle the high load of read qu=
eries this implementation would require. An RDS could be used successfully =
if done as a batch operation, but that would not align with the requirement=
 to return a real-time position for the user on the leaderboard.</p>
<p>One optimization we can do is to add an index and limit the number of pa=
ges to scan with the LIMIT clause. The query looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>SELECT (@rownum :=3D @rownum + 1) AS rank, user_id, score
FROM leaderboard
ORDER BY score DESC
LIMIT 10
</code></pre>
<p>However, this approach doesn=E2=80=99t scale well. First, finding a user=
's rank is not performant because it essentially requires a table scan to d=
etermine the rank. Second, this approach doesn=E2=80=99t provide a straight=
forward solution for determining the rank of a user who is not at the top o=
f the leaderboard.</p>
<h4 id=3D"redis-solution">Redis solution</h4>
<p>We want to find a solution that gives us predictable performance even fo=
r millions of users and allows us to have easy access to common leaderboard=
 operations, without needing to fall back on complex DB queries.</p>
<p>Redis provides a potential solution to our problem. Redis is an in-memor=
y data store supporting key-value pairs. Since it works in memory, it allow=
s for fast reads and writes. Redis has a specific data type called <strong>=
sorted sets</strong> that are ideal for solving leaderboard system design p=
roblems.</p>
<h5 id=3D"what-are-sorted-sets">What are sorted sets?</h5>
<p>A sorted set is a data type similar to a set. Each member of a sorted se=
t is associated with a score. The members of a set must be unique, but scor=
es may repeat. The score is used to rank the sorted set in ascending order.=
</p>
<p>Our leaderboard use case maps perfectly to sorted sets. Internally, a so=
rted set is implemented by two data structures: a hash table and a skip lis=
t [1]. The hash table maps users to scores and the skip list maps scores to=
 users. In sorted sets, users are sorted by scores. A good way to understan=
d a sorted set is to picture it as a table with score and member columns as=
 shown in Figure 8. The table is sorted by score in descending order.</p>
