<p>This works, but it runs into issues at a high load. DynamoDB splits data=
 across multiple nodes using consistent hashing. Each item lives in a corre=
sponding node based on its partition key. We want to structure the data so =
that data is evenly distributed across partitions. In our table design (Fig=
ure 23), all the data for the most recent month would be stored in one part=
ition and that partition becomes a hot partition. How can we solve this pro=
blem?</p>
<p>We can split data into <em>N</em> partitions and append a partition numb=
er (user_id % number_of_partitions) to the partition key. This pattern is c=
alled write sharding. Write sharding adds complexity for both read and writ=
e operations, so we should consider the trade-offs carefully.</p>
<p>The second question we need to answer is, how many partitions should we =
have? It can be based on write volume or DAU. The important thing to rememb=
er is that there is a trade-off between load on partitions and read complex=
ity. Because data for the same month is spread evenly across multiple parti=
tions, the load for a single partition is much lighter. However, to read it=
ems for a given month, we have to query all the partitions and merge the re=
sults, which adds read complexity.</p>
<p>The partition key looks something like this: =E2=80=9C<em>game_name#{yea=
r-month}#p{partition_number}</em>=E2=80=9D. Figure 24 shows the updated sch=
ema table.</p>
