g&amp;w=3D3840&amp;q=3D75" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div><div style=
=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Fig=
ure 24 Updated partition key</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>The global secondary index uses =E2=80=9C<em>game_name#{year-month}#p{pa=
rtition_number}</em>=E2=80=9D as the partition key and the score as the sor=
t key. What we end up with are <em>N</em> partitions that are all sorted wi=
thin their own partition (locally sorted). If we assume we had 3 partitions=
, then in order to fetch the top 10 leaderboard, we would use the approach =
called =E2=80=9Cscatter-gather=E2=80=9D mentioned earlier. We would fetch t=
he top 10 results in each of the partitions (this is the =E2=80=9Cscatter=
=E2=80=9D portion), and then we would allow the application to sort the res=
ults among all the partitions (this is the =E2=80=9Cgather=E2=80=9D portion=
). An example is shown in Figure 25.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a system for generating a top 10 leaderboar=
